Freeze investigation (docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md, docs/load-test-modules.md):
a real, repeated, unresolved hard-freeze under heavy CPU+GPU load (Minecraft,
specifically at the world-loading -> gameplay transition). Three independent
real repros left zero forensic trace anywhere (pstore/ramoops, the pseudo-NMI
hardlockup detector, CONFIG_DETECT_HUNG_TASK at a 5s timeout) - consistent
with a genuine software deadlock rather than a hardware/bus-level hang, since
none of the tools built to catch either of those ever fired.

A userspace capture script (tools/loadtest/capture_watch.py, fsync'd to real
storage rather than pstore's small DRAM region) caught the actual lead-up on
three separate real crashes: a rapid-fire sequence of genuine GPU faults
([drm:a6xx_irq] gpu fault -> [drm:recover_worker] hangcheck recover!),
recovering successfully several times in a row (fence counters climbing,
rptr/wptr resyncing each time) before the final attempt in each run showed a
GPU-IRQ-count spike far beyond any prior window, immediately followed by
total silence - once with the final recovery's own rptr/wptr never fully
resyncing before the log stopped.

Checked a sibling porting project's own real diagnosis for the same GPU
family (ubuntu-galaxy-tab-s9-ultra, docs/gpu-recovery.md) and confirmed the
exact same code, line-for-line, against this project's own tree: a real
circular-lock deadlock exists between GPU fault recovery and fault-capture
completion. `recover_worker()`/`msm_gpu_fault_crashstate_capture()`
(drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gpu.c) both need `gpu->lock`. `a6xx_gmu_set_oob()`
and `a6xx_hfi_wait_for_msg_interrupt()` can each be reached from inside
`recover_worker()` while it already holds `gpu->lock`, and both do an
*unbounded* `wait_for_completion(&a6xx_gpu->base.fault_coredump_done)` - but
the only code path that ever completes that completion
(`msm_gpu_fault_crashstate_capture()`, called from the SMMU fault IRQ thread)
itself starts with `mutex_lock(&gpu->lock)`. Recovery holds the lock and
waits forever for a completion that can only be signaled by code needing the
same lock.

Confirmed via online research this is a known, acknowledged-but-unfixed gap:
the `fault_coredump_done` mechanism was introduced by a 2025-07-18 freedreno
patch ("[PATCH] drm/msm: Wait for MMU devcoredump when waiting for GMU").
During its own review, the GMU maintainer (Akhil P Oommen) flagged this exact
scenario verbatim: "we might be holding both gpu and gmu locks here and the
crashstate capture in the pf handler tries to lock gpu, which can result in a
dead lock." The author acknowledged the risk but no fix followed - the patch
merged anyway, and no later revision or follow-up thread addresses it. Still
present, unfixed, in this v7.2-based tree.

No upstream fix exists to backport, so this bounds both wait loops to one
timed retry (1s) instead of waiting forever - the same fix the S9 Ultra
project independently arrived at for the same underlying bug on a different
device. Preserves the existing behavior on the success path entirely; only
changes what happens when a fault capture is genuinely stuck. Not a claim to
fix the initial GPU fault itself - only to stop recovery from deadlocking on
top of it.

Status: built, flashed, and tested on real hardware (2026-09-21) - see
docs/load-test-modules.md ("Fix attempt: bounded fault_coredump_done wait")
for the full test log. Device still froze, but the failure's observable
signature changed: instead of instant silent death, this run captured a
single CPU core pinning at 100% and staying there, unchanging, for over
1.5 continuous seconds before the system died - direct, sustained evidence
of a spin never seen this clearly before. However, this patch's own new
error path (`DRM_DEV_ERROR("Timeout waiting for GMU OOB...")`) never fired
during that window, despite dozens of opportunities to catch it - so this
is NOT confirmed to be the actual root cause. Either a different, still-
unbounded loop is the real culprit, or something downstream of this fix's
new timeout return doesn't handle the failure gracefully and spins
elsewhere instead. Keep this fix in place (it's still a real improvement -
recovery no longer waits forever on a stuck completion), but the freeze
itself remains unresolved and needs further investigation targeting
whatever is actually spinning.

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
index 2e5d7b53a..5e587bf65 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
@@ -395,6 +395,7 @@ int a6xx_gmu_set_oob(struct a6xx_gmu *gmu, enum a6xx_gmu_oob_state state)
 	struct a6xx_gpu *a6xx_gpu = container_of(gmu, struct a6xx_gpu, gmu);
 	struct adreno_gpu *adreno_gpu = &a6xx_gpu->base;
 	int ret;
+	bool waited_for_fault = false;
 	u32 val;
 	int request, ack;

@@ -432,14 +433,25 @@ int a6xx_gmu_set_oob(struct a6xx_gmu *gmu, enum a6xx_gmu_oob_state state)
 		if (!ret)
 			break;

-		if (completion_done(&a6xx_gpu->base.fault_coredump_done))
+		if (waited_for_fault ||
+		    completion_done(&a6xx_gpu->base.fault_coredump_done))
 			break;

-		/* We may timeout because the GMU is temporarily wedged from
-		 * pending faults from the GPU and we are taking a devcoredump.
-		 * Wait until the MMU is resumed and try again.
+		/*
+		 * gts7l freeze investigation (docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md):
+		 * recover_worker()/msm_gpu_fault_crashstate_capture() both
+		 * need gpu->lock, and this OOB wait can be reached while
+		 * recovery already holds it - an unbounded
+		 * wait_for_completion() here can deadlock both paths
+		 * (flagged by the GMU maintainer during this completion's
+		 * own upstream review, freedreno list msg39107/39108, never
+		 * fixed). Bound the wait to one retry so a stuck fault
+		 * capture times out instead of hanging forever.
 		 */
-		wait_for_completion(&a6xx_gpu->base.fault_coredump_done);
+		waited_for_fault = true;
+		if (!wait_for_completion_timeout(&a6xx_gpu->base.fault_coredump_done,
+						  msecs_to_jiffies(1000)))
+			break;
 	} while (true);

 	if (ret)
